# nextjs-fsd

This skill is repository-wide. It applies to any package in this repository
that has a `nextjs-fsd.config.json`; the package's own `AGENTS.md` and
`docs/fsd.md` hold the exact paths, import alias, and product-specific rules.
This file only explains how to drive the generator and where the boundaries
are.

## Find the target package first

1. Locate the nearest `nextjs-fsd.config.json` from the directory the user
   means. If the repository has several package configs, do not guess: name the
   candidates and ask which package the change belongs to.
2. Read that config. `srcDir` is where the FSD layers live; `appDir` says
   whether routes live in `app/` or `src/app/`; `features.errorHandling` and
   `features.auth` decide which flags are legal; `locale` says which language
   generated copy uses; and the import alias is recorded there.
3. Read the target package's `AGENTS.md` and `docs/fsd.md`. If they disagree
   with this skill, the package docs are the source of truth for that package.
4. Run every command from the target package's root, not from the repository
   root.

## FSD theory comes from its own skill

This file covers only how to drive the generator and where the boundaries are.
For what a layer, slice, or segment is for — and whether something should be an
entity at all — defer to the `feature-sliced-design` skill that `init` wrote
alongside this one. Guessing at the shape is how a plausible wrong slice happens.

## Commands

Pass every required value explicitly: an omitted value opens a prompt, and in a
non-interactive shell that exits 1 without writing anything.

~~~bash
nextjs-fsd generate page <name...> [--title <t>] [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--client] [--auth] [--api] [--errors] [-r <root>] [--defaults]
nextjs-fsd generate slice <features|entities|widgets> <name...> [--segments <list>] [-r <root>] [--errors] [--defaults]
nextjs-fsd generate layout <name> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--guard] [--defaults]
nextjs-fsd generate api-route <name> [--route <path>] [--no-route] [--defaults]
nextjs-fsd add error-handling [-y] [--no-install]
nextjs-fsd add auth [-y] [--no-install]
nextjs-fsd add prettier [-y] [--no-install]
nextjs-fsd config show
~~~

`--defaults` answers every remaining question, so scripted and agent use needs
`--defaults` (or the full set of flags) on every generate command.

Feature gates are deliberate:

- `--auth` requires `features.auth`; run `add auth` first.
- `--guard` requires `features.auth` too; it puts every route under a layout
  behind the session.
- `--errors` and the `api` segment require `features.errorHandling`; run
  `add error-handling` first.
- `add auth` installs error handling itself when it is missing.
- `add prettier` formats the whole package once on purpose; commit that pass
  separately.

## Extend, do not rewrite

Re-run the same command to add to an existing slice. Do not hand-write files
the CLI owns, and do not delete a slice to regenerate it.

Only missing files are written; new exports are appended; existing files are
left alone. If there is nothing to add, the command says so and writes nothing
— that is the expected answer, not a failure to work around.

## Contracts that fail silently

- A route file re-exports the page and its `metadata`. Dropping `metadata`
  silently drops the page title.
- `"use client"` goes on the leaf, not the page. A page stays a server
  component; state, effects, and events live in `ui/<thing>.tsx`.
- A page with a client leaf has two public API entries: `index.ts` for the
  client-safe leaf and `index.server.ts` for the server component and
  `metadata`. A Client Component must never pull the server module into its
  graph.
- A `route.ts` re-exports the handler as the HTTP method and nothing else.
  `middleware.js` and `instrumentation.js` stay at the target package's root.
- Imports point downwards only, and a slice is entered through its public API
  (`index.ts`, or `index.server.ts` for server-only modules). Inside a slice,
  use relative imports.
- Never render `error.message` as UI copy. Add the code to the domain's own
  catalog (`model/<name>-errors.ts`) and let the fallback name the action that
  failed.
- One catalog per domain, never one global map.
- A failure the user must fix stays inline; one they only need to know goes to
  a toast. `<FormError>` is the inline half.
- A second consumer that shares data usually means `<srcDir>/shared/<domain>/`,
  not a new `entities/` slice, until a business rule lands in that segment.
- If the package locale is Thai, no negative tracking, nothing Thai inside
  `font-mono`, and line heights loosened per text size; tone marks stack and a
  mono face loaded with the latin subset has no Thai glyph.
- The access token stays in the module variable in
  `<srcDir>/shared/auth/access-token.ts`; never move it to `localStorage`, and
  never remove the single-flight guard in `<srcDir>/shared/api/client.ts`.
- The configured app directory composes pages, not features. ESLint enforces
  this.

## Auth and error handling

The generated auth/error code is a starting adapter, not a mandate. Read the
package's `<srcDir>/shared/api/` and `<srcDir>/shared/auth/` before changing
it. If the backend does not match the default response shape, keep the
package's public client contract and replace the adapter; do not copy a second
client.

A 401 from `/auth/*` means wrong credentials or an expired challenge; do not
refresh it. A 401 that survives one refresh means the session is over,
regardless of which request found it.

## Verification

Run the target package's own scripts, not a generic command from the repository
root:

~~~bash
<package manager> run lint
<package manager> run build
~~~

Run the package's test command too. Both linters report a wrong-way or
slice-internal import: ESLint per file, Steiger over the whole tree. Steiger
also reports what no single file can show, such as an unimported slice. Report
the exact output and say which generated TODOs are still TODOs.
